---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  navKey="privacy"
  title="Privacy"
  description="Deptheos collects no telemetry or analytics: your photos stay on your device, the only network request is the desktop update check, and nothing tracks you."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Deptheos collects nothing about you. Your photos, renders, and logs stay on your own computer, iPhone, or
      iPad. This page says exactly what the apps send over the network—at most one request, the desktop update
      check—and what happens to diagnostics when you choose to send them.
    </p>
    <p>
      This policy covers the Deptheos desktop app for macOS, Windows and Linux, the command line tool
      (<code>deptheos</code>), the iPhone and iPad app, and this website. Last updated 20 November 2026.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="photos">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Your photos never leave your device</h2>
      <p>
        Stacking, retouching and exporting all happen on your own device, on its GPU. Your frames are read where
        they are and never uploaded anywhere. The Library (your stacks, renders and retouching), the app's
        settings and its logs are kept in folders on the same device. There is no account to sign in to and no
        cloud service behind the apps.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="network">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What goes over the network</h2>
      <h3 id="update-check">The desktop app: the update check</h3>
      <p>
        The desktop app makes one kind of request: it asks GitHub whether a newer version of Deptheos has been
        released. It asks once each time it starts, unless you turn off Settings ▸ General ▸
        <em>Check for updates</em>, and whenever you choose Check for Updates… (in the Deptheos menu on a Mac,
        the Help menu on Windows and Linux).
      </p>
      <p>
        The request is one HTTPS request for Deptheos's latest release:
      </p>
      <p><code class="cmd">GET https://api.github.com/repos/ryanmitts/deptheos/releases/latest</code></p>
      <p>
        It carries the app's version in its User-Agent header (<code>Deptheos/0.2.0</code>, for example, as
        GitHub requires every request to name its client) and nothing else: no identifier, no system details,
        nothing about your photos or how you use the app. The app reads only the latest release's version
        number from the reply. Nothing is downloaded or installed: when a newer version is out, Download opens
        its release page in your browser. As with any request, GitHub sees the address it came from;
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's
        privacy statement</a> covers what GitHub does with that.
      </p>
      <h3 id="ios-network">The iPhone and iPad app</h3>
      <p>
        The iPhone and iPad app makes no network requests of its own. When you add photos that are kept in
        iCloud, iOS downloads them to your device for the app, as it would for any app.
      </p>
      <h3 id="cli-network">The command line tool</h3>
      <p>The command line tool makes no network requests at all.</p>
      <h3 id="links">Links</h3>
      <p>
        Menu items and buttons that name a web page (the docs, the release notes, the source code, Report a
        Problem…) open that page in your browser. The app itself sends nothing when it does.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostics">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Diagnostics, only when you send them</h2>
      <p>
        The apps keep a log on your device to help with bug reports. It names the files and folders the app
        read, as a log must to be any use, with your home folder written as <code>~</code>. It is never sent
        anywhere by the app.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you choose Help ▸ <em>Save Diagnostics…</em> on the desktop, or Settings ▸ Diagnostics ▸
        <em>Send Diagnostics…</em> on iPhone and iPad, the app makes one zip file of its recent logs, and on the
        desktop its crash reports from the last 30 days. Your home folder is written as <code>~</code> in the logs
        and a Mac's crash reports, whose machine identifiers are blanked out too. A crash dump on Windows is a
        copy of part of the app's memory at the moment it crashed, which can't be cleaned that way. The file is
        yours: it goes only where you send it. If you email it to <a href="mailto:support@deptheos.com">support@deptheos.com</a>, it is used only
        to look into the problem you reported, and is not shared with anyone. <a href="/support/">Support</a>
        says how to send it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tracking">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>No tracking</h2>
      <p>
        The apps have no analytics, no advertising, no crash reporting service and no third-party code that
        sends data. The App Store lists the iPhone and iPad app as <em>Data Not Collected</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        This website sets no cookies and runs no analytics or tracking scripts; its fonts are served from the
        site itself. It is hosted on Cloudflare, which keeps the usual records of the requests it serves, as any
        web host does.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="changes">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Changes and questions</h2>
      <p>
        If a later version of Deptheos changes anything here, this page changes with it before that version is
        released, and its date above changes too. Deptheos is open source, so what the apps send can be checked
        in <a href="https://github.com/ryanmitts/deptheos">the source code</a>. Questions about privacy go to
        <a href="mailto:support@deptheos.com">support@deptheos.com</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
